g i noticed  occurred quite frequently in brandon's  writing and this kind of goes hand in  hand with the everyday  modern language modern sounding writers  will use the m dash  more frequently so what's the m dash  it's just the end dash but longer  right it's just a hyphen but longer  usually it's used  to replace a comma and to make it more  just visually separated from a sentence  so you have a long  sentence dependent clauses and all this  stuff and you want to  pull out a certain piece of information  or if it's if it's an aside you can use  this too  brandon will use it to add clarity the  m-dash  really just helps organize the  information in a sentence brandon uses  really simple everyday sentence  structure the m-dash helps him do that  for example  a landlord was encroaching on breitlord  amaram's territory  the owned ultimately by high prince  sadius in some ways that's better than  akama because  it really puts significance on the  exposition  high prince edius it's owned ultimately  by high prince edius also it just  looks and feels better because he  already has the ultimately there in  commas  i think that looks a little better and i  think it sounds better too  the next thing a stylistic quirk that  sanderson uses he uses lots of sentence  fragments  along with repetition you'll find this a  lot in  important scenes emotional scenes  instead of going  into wordy descriptions of feelings or  things he'll repeat phrases  or add a sentence fragment i'm not  saying one is more effective than the  other and a lot of people would  criticize sanderson for using this maybe  thinking it's shallow or something i  think that  it can be a really effective thing  especially for the style he's going for  which is  peeled back amaram soldiers raised  shields  but not kaladin's squad no shields for  them sen screamed  and the arrow slammed into the middle  ranks of amaram's army but not caledon  squad  no shields for them helps with the pace  right it helps and it'