nce for the murder of king gavilar  five years before  okay we have a character asking a normal  question and then  perhaps right it's connect us to the  prologue  to the book just also giving this  exposition and reminding us  about how much time has passed since the  prologue it's been five years and  not only in in turn internally does  brandon sanderson do this but  of course he does it in the dialogue  when sen asks dalet how did you know  about the arrows and he gives the answer  you know that is  a little more common and probably  typical for exposition brandon's just  very good at using that  intern those internal mechanisms to get  exposition across  so after looking at all these qualities  of his writing  obviously there's a lot you could pick  apart and dissect and try to figure out  but  after looking at these things does  brandon sanderson have a prominent  style yeah you know i i do think that he  has a style  it's a style that doesn't try to stick  out in certain ways  but it does stick out in others right it  sticks out in  its use of sentence fragments of  repetition of certain phrases  italics for particular words for  emphasis so there is a style there but  yeah it doesn't reveal much  beyond what brandon sanderson wants to  reveal  it's very to the point and it's very  economical these are long books  the reason they expand so so much has  more to do with scene construction  than it does with the pros style it's  not invisible i don't think any writer  style is invisible  the question is if it's effective and  good i think that really comes down to  the reader i think  largely it doesn't get in the way of the  story i think the story gets in the way  of the story sometimes  i think that writers can learn a lot  from this style because  sanderson excels at what a lot of  writers  don't which is getting to the point  cleaning up  the pros and getting down to the very  essential stuff yeah what does everybody  think about brandon sanderson's  style 